      "role": "user",
      "content": "Actually, I'm pretty sure you're misinterpreting the term \"residuals\" here. In legal documents like NDAs, \"residuals\" usually refers to the information that remains in a person's memory after they've seen confidential data—it's a standard way to carve out an exception so you don't get sued just for being smarter after the job is done. \n\nIf the clause says I am \"free to use\" them, it sounds like a protection for me, not a trap for the client. \n\nBut let's get back to the technical limit: if I use a coding technique that I simply remember—not a copy of their code, but just the *logic*—how would a legal team even prove I'm not using \"unaided memory\"? It seems like they'd have to prove I'm carrying around some kind of tangible \"secret\" when it's just my own professional expertise. Is there any specific way they'd try to bridge that gap between my memory and their property?",
